<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Garonne</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Garonne is a major river of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, about 530 km long, rising in the Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a> and flowing past <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a> to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dordogne">Dordogne</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gironde-estuary">Gironde estuary</a>. In antiquity it marked the boundary between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquitani">Aquitani</a> and the Celtic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a>, and it has long carried trade between the Atlantic and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> by way of Toulouse.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/garonne.jpg" alt="Garonne" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Oliwan · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bordeaux_port_de_la_lune_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By about 1100 the counts of Toulouse also claimed the titles of duke of Narbonne and marquis of Provence, which made them the most powerful lords between the <b>Garonne</b> and the Rhône.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-toulouse">County of Toulouse · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 418 Honorius rewarded his federates under King Wallia (415–418) with land in the <b>Garonne</b> valley of Gallia Aquitania.</blockquote><a href="/empire/visigothic-kingdom">Visigothic Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Strabo notes that the Bituriges Vivisci were the only Celtic people living among the Aquitani, which suggests they migrated from the Bituriges Cubi of Berry to the lower <b>Garonne</b>, perhaps in the 3rd or 2nd century BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/bituriges-vivisci">Bituriges Vivisci</a></li><li><blockquote>According to Jerome, around 72 BCE Pompey, returning from his war against Sertorius in Hispania, settled defeated fighters and mountain people at Lugdunum in the upper <b>Garonne</b> valley.</blockquote><a href="/state/convenae">Convenae</a></li><li><blockquote>Counts of Comminges appear in the early 10th century, governing the upper <b>Garonne</b> valley around the old Roman town of Lugdunum Convenarum, later Saint-Bertrand-de-Comminges.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-comminges">County of Comminges</a></li><li><blockquote>The Nitiobroges lived on the <b>Garonne</b> around Aginnum (Agen), at the border between the Celtic peoples and the Aquitani.</blockquote><a href="/state/nitiobroges">Nitiobroges</a></li></ul></section>
